# Goal: explain whether one systemd service is consuming resources through its
# workers or child processes, rather than looking only at MainPID.
#
# Inputs and environment: an exact systemd unit name, a Linux systemd manager,
# readable cgroup v2 state, and /proc. Replace the unit with a service that is
# meaningful on the target host; systemd-journald.service is only an example.
#
# Order: yesh asks systemd for the exact unit and ControlGroup, reads the cgroup
# subtree, then joins its processes with /proc and prints one bounded snapshot.
#
# Changes: none. The operation is read-only; it does not start, stop, signal,
# throttle, or otherwise modify the service or its resource limits.
#
# Failure and boundary: a missing unit, denied access, unavailable cgroup, or a
# process disappearing during the read is reported or marked unavailable. The
# result describes one moment and does not prove future load or explain the
# cause of an incident by itself.
#
# Role of yesh: keep the unit identity, cgroup membership, process fields, and
# resource counters connected and visible. systemd, cgroup v2, and /proc remain
# the sources of state.
service resources systemd-journald.service
